\begin_layout Section
Acoustic analysis of a reduced electro-traditional duo (OSSO)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
An accordion playing in a reduced ensemble has to carry melody, harmony,
 a possible drone and the rhythmic drive of the dance with two sources only.
 The Breton duo OSSO transposes the traditional biniou/bombarde couple to
 analogue synthesisers, which makes it a useful control case for the sharing
 of roles discussed in this part: the functions are the same, the sound
 production is not.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Two pieces were analysed (Ordre etant, hanter-dro, 296.9 s; Duderc'h,
 scottish, 240.4 s), through mid/side decomposition, spectral descriptors,
 a per-band envelope modulation spectrum and Praat measurements, computed
 with librosa 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
after ""
before ""
key "mcfee2015librosa"
literal "true"

\end_inset

 and parselmouth 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
after ""
before ""
key "jadoul2018parselmouth"
literal "true"

\end_inset

; key estimation follows the Krumhansl-Schmuckler profiles 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
after ""
before ""
key "krumhansl1990"
literal "true"

\end_inset

.
 Three results are relevant to accordion design.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Stereophony encodes the functional split rather than a mere widening: the
 mid/side energy ratio reaches 9.2 below 60 Hz (a strictly centred monophonic
 bass) and drops to 0.76 in the 1-2 kHz band (a spatialised mid-range).
 The traditional couple separated the same roles by register and timbre.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Amplitude modulations are locked to the metric grid within 0.1 to 0.3 percent
 over four distinct subdivisions (0.52, 1.57, 3.13 and 6.27 Hz against a
 94.0 BPM beat): tremolo and filter movements act as rhythmic agents.
 The modulation spectrum also gives an independent check on the metric level,
 and corrects a metric octave error of the beat tracker 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
after ""
before ""
key "ellis2007beat"
literal "true"

\end_inset

 on the second piece: 120.2 BPM estimated, 90.0 BPM confirmed.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
A progressive filter opening structures one piece only: spectral centroid
 slope +5.17 Hz/s, coefficient of determination 0.298, p below 0.001, against
 -0.33 Hz/s (not significant) for the other.
 Timbre alone carries a directional dramaturgy over a cyclic dance form,
 without touching the melodic material or the metre.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The measured continuity of the texture (crest factor 10.8 and 13.2 dB,
 dynamic range 10.5 and 7.9 dB) is obtained here by compression, where the
 traditional instruments obtain it from the air reserve of the bag and the
 accordion from its bellows.
 The parallel suggests two transposable controls for the accordion player:
 locking any periodic modulation (alternating basses, bellows effects) to a
 subdivision of the beat, and using registers, bellows pressure and
 accompaniment density as a trajectory rather than as an alternation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The complete study, with all tables, statistics and methodological limits,
 is kept in French in research/docs/analyse_acoustique_osso.md; the
 reproducible script is research/scripts/analyse_osso.py.
 The recordings themselves are not distributed with this work (copyright);
 only derived acoustic descriptors are reported.
\end_layout

